3. THE ATTACK THAT SURVIVED THE FIRST FIX, AND WHAT IT COST TO CLOSE
--------------------------------------------------------------------

With `bindSuite`, `atTest` and `defineEvidenceCapture` all derived and all converted to consts, this
still compiled CLEAN, measured in isolation:

  async (ctx: AtContext<NotificationsSut & { invented?: string }, World>) => {
    const { sut } = await ctx.open();
    void sut.invented;                       // string | undefined; `undefined` at run time
  }

$ node ... -p (isolated single-attack config)
EXIT=0

Widening happens at the BODY, not at the entry point, so closing the entry points did not reach it.
And nothing structural can: `NotificationsSut` and `NotificationsSut & { invented?: string }` are
assignable in BOTH directions â€” an optional member adds no obligation â€” so no variance annotation
and no conditional "exactly this type" trick can distinguish them.

The only close is to make the widened type UNSPELLABLE. `OpenWorld`, `AtContext`, `AtTestBody` and
`EvidenceCapture` are now exported over `<R, K>` â€” a requirement id and a sut key, both of which
derive â€” and the shape-parameterized versions stay module-private. The attack is line 72 above.

This is the item's own lesson applied to itself: the first version would have shipped claiming a
closure it did not have, and only running the compiler showed it.


